#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12276898-9979-57a0-8ed0-2ba59a641ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,2⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263170} {:answer/id #uuid "154fa3a3-16f8-55a0-846e-51231182efb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,3⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263198} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4bb37a-bda2-5a72-a223-f0c5b9c34121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨2,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264193} {:answer/id #uuid "c17bef72-8988-5da9-b2e6-8b78f6621cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264680} {:answer/id #uuid "f580560e-dc14-5b52-a0f5-5aaae87a83f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c17bef72-8988-5da9-b2e6-8b78f6621cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264680} :answer-field/id #uuid "60641db1-7e26-5e36-8c32-48b2fb158832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "256d5ce8-b202-5957-9fe3-266dd8fdafa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132208" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e9cdc8-9841-5f0d-ab5a-8833d16224d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127100} {:answer/id #uuid "93552b22-934c-541e-af6f-55949ee3eb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127101} {:answer/id #uuid "ad44e94e-055c-5eb9-af28-414c9a6aff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127102} {:answer/id #uuid "703cf7cc-9888-5857-b179-d931e8128941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127103} {:answer/id #uuid "70751244-3954-52d4-81d1-a9cbbad5fd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127104}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fe156952-6c9e-51ed-a0eb-ed79cba1476f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b22824-daf4-5d0e-b5e9-203cb0249071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382041} {:answer/id #uuid "8033de03-7e63-5b27-9a40-3b6ef01194d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382042} {:answer/id #uuid "de1a6139-5d33-5664-9017-a573aaa19ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382043} {:answer/id #uuid "33af0c6d-3453-5b4f-868b-dc798d347c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382044} {:answer/id #uuid "30ea66e6-af09-5377-9e27-ac3aee6ef9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30ea66e6-af09-5377-9e27-ac3aee6ef9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382045} :answer-field/id #uuid "452b2833-f8aa-57bb-843b-3f3e95179f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcfe0e97-f702-5d83-9f81-8d21ad944be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36949" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7(\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18510c8b-e669-5011-aa0f-5f9e17e08d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333206} {:answer/id #uuid "756ded08-1584-5e69-aa8a-0f9ea158b9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334049} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7c7fd3-e1ab-54db-88ef-745e0c891aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334526} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b9b11-3257-5dca-a1de-29e8d4614542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7t}-2t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334533} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b7cca8-117d-5882-8808-4eb7ab006ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{7t}-4t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae7c7fd3-e1ab-54db-88ef-745e0c891aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334526} :answer-field/id #uuid "853ffbce-93a2-5e36-a152-729501632660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41fb4ba9-2a2a-520b-a6ad-5cdc344e0ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-64798" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution."}]}
